What 04-rejection-fake-event.json demonstrates: replay-protected event substitution.

The file name and its "FALSE-EVENT" label were written when the attempt was run. They
describe the intent, not the gate that fired, and are kept unedited as the raw record.

The attempt took the InvoicePaid transaction already verified in 02-invoice-paid.json
(0x6d909c47...) and asked the receiver to read it as InvoiceDefaulted instead. ASCReceiver
rejected it at gate 1 with AlreadyProcessed, before any event decoding, because the replay key
is derived from the proof itself (chainKey, blockHeight, txIndex) and not from the event the
caller names.

The property shown: a processed proof cannot be reused to reinterpret the same source
transaction as a different event. CreditFile was unchanged (downstreamUnchanged: true).

What it does not show: the emitter authorization gate. That gate never ran here, because
gate 1 stops the call first. Its live rejection is evidence/mainnet/ (UnauthorizedSource, a
real Ethereum mainnet transaction whose event came from WETH rather than the authorized
USDC), and test/ASCSecurity.t.sol covers it in test_gate6_unauthorizedEmitterRejected.

Both rejections were caught at gas estimation, which executes the full call and reverts with
the contract's reason before anything is broadcast (worker/worker.mjs, spike/mainnet-probe.mjs).
Neither was mined, so neither has a Creditcoin transaction to open. The reasons are recorded
here and in evidence/mainnet/README.md.
